    question: "What are the primitive types in TypeScript?",
    idealAnswer: "TypeScript provides several primitive types that represent the most basic kinds of values:\n\n**Core Primitive Types:**\n- `string`: Textual data enclosed in quotes\n- `number`: All numeric values (integers, floats, NaN, Infinity)\n- `boolean`: Logical values true or false\n- `null`: Intentional absence of any object value\n- `undefined`: Variable declared but not assigned a value\n- `symbol`: Unique and immutable primitive values\n- `bigint`: Large integers beyond Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER\n\n**Examples:**\n```typescript\nlet name: string = \"John Doe\";\nlet age: number = 25;\nlet isActive: boolean = true;\nlet empty: null = null;\nlet notAssigned: undefined = undefined;\nlet unique: symbol = Symbol(\"id\");\nlet hugeNumber: bigint = 9007199254740991n;\n```\n\n**Special Cases:**\n```typescript\n// Number includes special values\nlet specialNum: number = NaN;        // Not a Number\nlet infinity: number = Infinity;    // Positive infinity\nlet negInfinity: number = -Infinity; // Negative infinity\n\n// String types\nlet singleQuote: string = 'hello';\nlet doubleQuote: string = \"hello\";\nlet template: string = `hello ${name}`;\n```\n\n**Type Checking:**\n```typescript\n// typeof operator works with primitives\ntypeof \"hello\"; // \"string\"\ntypeof 42;       // \"number\"\ntypeof true;     // \"boolean\"\ntypeof null;     // \"object\" (historical JavaScript quirk)\ntypeof undefined; // \"undefined\"\n```"
