    question: "What is the any type?",
    idealAnswer: "**The `any` type** is a special type in TypeScript that represents a value of any type. It essentially opts out of type checking for that variable.\n\n**Characteristics of `any`:**\n- Can hold any value (string, number, object, function, etc.)\n- Allows any operations without type checking\n- Can be assigned to any other type\n- Provides no type safety or IntelliSense\n\n**Basic Usage:**\n```typescript\nlet value: any = 42;\nvalue = \"hello\";      // OK\nvalue = [1, 2, 3];    // OK\nvalue = () => {};     // OK\n\n// No type checking\nvalue.foo.bar;        // No error, but may crash at runtime\nvalue();             // No error, but may crash at runtime\nvalue[0][1];         // No error, but may crash at runtime\n```\n\n**Assignment Compatibility:**\n```typescript\nlet anyValue: any = \"hello\";\nlet str: string = anyValue;  // OK - any can be assigned to any type\nlet num: number = anyValue;  // OK\nlet bool: boolean = anyValue; // OK\n```\n\n**When to Use `any`:**\n```typescript\n// 1. Migrating from JavaScript to TypeScript\nfunction legacyFunction(data: any) {\n  // Handle dynamic data from old code\n}\n\n// 2. Third-party libraries without type definitions\nimport thirdPartyLib from \"some-lib\";\nconst result: any = thirdPartyLib.process(data);\n\n// 3. Complex dynamic data structures\nfunction parseJSON(jsonString: string): any {\n  return JSON.parse(jsonString);\n}\n```\n\n**Risks of Using `any`:**\n- **No Type Safety**: Runtime errors that could have been caught\n- **Poor IDE Support**: No autocomplete or error checking\n- **Maintenance Issues**: Harder to understand and refactor\n- **Team Confusion**: Unclear what the variable should contain\n\n**Better Alternatives:**\n```typescript\n// Use unknown for type-safe dynamic values\nfunction processValue(value: unknown) {\n  if (typeof value === \"string\") {\n    // TypeScript knows value is string here\n    console.log(value.toUpperCase());\n  }\n}\n\n// Use specific union types\ntype StringOrNumber = string | number;\nfunction display(value: StringOrNumber) {\n  console.log(value);\n}\n```"
