    question: "What is unknown type?",
    idealAnswer: "**The `unknown` type** is a type-safe alternative to `any`. It represents any value but requires type checking before you can perform operations on it.\n\n**Key Characteristics:**\n- Can hold any value like `any`\n- **Type-safe**: Must be type-checked before use\n- Cannot be assigned to other types without checking\n- Provides better type safety and IDE support\n\n**Basic Usage:**\n```typescript\nlet value: unknown = 42;\n\n// These are all errors - must type-check first:\n// value.foo.bar; // Error: Object is of type 'unknown'\n// value();      // Error: Object is of type 'unknown'\n// value[0][1];  // Error: Object is of type 'unknown'\n\n// let num: number = value; // Error: Type 'unknown' is not assignable to type 'number'\n```\n\n**Type Checking with `unknown`:**\n```typescript\nfunction processValue(value: unknown) {\n  // Type narrowing with typeof\n  if (typeof value === \"string\") {\n    // TypeScript knows value is string here\n    console.log(value.toUpperCase()); // OK\n  }\n  \n  if (typeof value === \"number\") {\n    console.log(value.toFixed(2)); // OK\n  }\n  \n  // Type guard function\n  if (isArray(value)) {\n    console.log(value.length); // OK\n  }\n}\n\nfunction isArray(value: unknown): value is unknown[] {\n  return Array.isArray(value);\n}\n```\n\n**Assignment Rules:**\n```typescript\nlet unknownValue: unknown = \"hello\";\n\n// Cannot assign to specific types without checking\n// let str: string = unknownValue; // Error\n\n// But can assign unknown to any\nlet anyValue: any = unknownValue; // OK\n\n// Can assign any to unknown\nlet anotherUnknown: unknown = anyValue; // OK\n```\n\n**Real-world Example:**\n```typescript\n// API response handling\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nasync function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);\n  const data: unknown = await response.json();\n  \n  // Validate the data\n  if (isUser(data)) {\n    return data; // TypeScript knows this is User\n  }\n  \n  throw new Error(\"Invalid user data\");\n}\n\nfunction isUser(data: unknown): data is User {\n  return typeof data === \"object\" && \n         data !== null && \n         \"id\" in data && \n         \"name\" in data &&\n         typeof (data as any).id === \"number\" &&\n         typeof (data as any).name === \"string\";\n}\n```\n\n**When to Use `unknown`:**\n- API responses that need validation\n- Function parameters that accept multiple types\n- Migration from JavaScript (better than `any`)\n- Library code that needs to be type-safe\n\n**`unknown` vs `any` Comparison:**\n```typescript\n// any - no type safety\nfunction processAny(value: any) {\n  value.anyProperty.anyMethod(); // No error, but may crash\n}\n\n// unknown - type safe\nfunction processUnknown(value: unknown) {\n  // Must type-check before using\n  if (typeof value === \"object\" && value !== null) {\n    // Now safe to use as object\n  }\n}\n```"
