    question: "Difference between interface and type",
    idealAnswer: "**Interfaces and Types** are both ways to define type structures in TypeScript, but they have key differences in functionality and use cases.\n\n**Interface:**\n- **Extensible**: Can be extended and support declaration merging\n- **Object-oriented**: Better suited for classes and object shapes\n- **Inheritance**: Can extend other interfaces\n- **Readability**: More descriptive for API contracts\n\n**Type Alias:**\n- **Flexible**: Can represent primitives, unions, intersections, tuples\n- **Immutable**: Cannot be reopened or extended after declaration\n- **Utility-friendly**: Better for complex type transformations\n- **Expressive**: More powerful for advanced type operations\n\n**Interface Examples:**\n```typescript\n// Basic interface\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\n// Interface extension\ninterface Employee extends User {\n  id: number;\n  department: string;\n}\n\n// Declaration merging\ninterface User {\n  email: string;\n}\n// Result: User has name, age, and email\n```\n\n**Type Alias Examples:**\n```typescript\n// Basic type alias\ntype User = {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n};\n\n// Union type (interfaces can't do this)\ntype ID = string | number;\n\n// Intersection type\ntype UserWithEmail = User & {\n  email: string;\n};\n\n// Complex utility type\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>;\ntype UserKeys = keyof User;\n```\n\n**Key Differences:**\n```typescript\n// 1. Declaration Merging\ninterface Settings {\n  theme: string;\n}\ninterface Settings {\n  language: string;\n}\n// Settings has both theme and language\n\n// Type aliases cannot merge\ntype Config = { version: string };\n// type Config = { api: string }; // Error!\n\n// 2. Extending\ninterface Animal {\n  name: string;\n}\ninterface Dog extends Animal {\n  breed: string;\n}\n\n// Types use intersections for similar effect\ntype AnimalType = {\n  name: string;\n};\ntype DogType = AnimalType & {\n  breed: string;\n};\n\n// 3. Primitive representation\ntype StringOrNumber = string | number; // Only types can do this\n// interface StringOrNumber {} // Error!\n```\n\n**When to Use Each:**\n\n**Use Interface When:**\n- Defining object shapes and class contracts\n- Creating public APIs that might be extended\n- Working with class inheritance\n- Need declaration merging\n- Traditional object-oriented programming\n\n**Use Type Alias When:**\n- Working with union types, intersections, or tuples\n- Creating utility types and complex transformations\n- Need to represent primitive types\n- Working with conditional types\n- Advanced type manipulation\n\n**Performance Considerations:**\n- Interfaces are generally faster for the TypeScript compiler\n- Types with complex operations may be slower\n- For most applications, the difference is negligible\n\n**Best Practice:**\n- Start with interfaces for object shapes\n- Use types for unions, intersections, and utilities\n- Be consistent within your codebase\n- Consider team familiarity and project requirements"
