    question: "What is declaration merging?",
    idealAnswer: "**Declaration merging** is a TypeScript feature that allows the compiler to merge multiple declarations with the same name into a single definition. This is particularly useful for extending existing types and interfaces.\n\n**Interface Merging:**\n```typescript\n// First declaration\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\n// Second declaration - gets merged\ninterface User {\n  email: string;\n  phone?: string;\n}\n\n// Result: User has all properties\nconst user: User = {\n  name: \"John\",\n  age: 30,\n  email: \"john@example.com\",\n  phone: \"123-456-7890\"\n};\n```\n\n**Function Merging:**\n```typescript\n// Function overloading through declaration merging\nfunction greet(name: string): void;\nfunction greet(name: string, age: number): void;\n\nfunction greet(name: string, age?: number): void {\n  if (age) {\n    console.log(`Hello ${name}, you are ${age} years old`);\n  } else {\n    console.log(`Hello ${name}`);\n  }\n}\n\ngreet(\"Alice\");           // \"Hello Alice\"\ngreet(\"Bob\", 25);         // \"Hello Bob, you are 25\"\n```\n\n**Namespace Merging:**\n```typescript\n// First namespace declaration\nnamespace MyLib {\n  export function greet(name: string): void {\n    console.log(`Hello, ${name}`);\n  }\n}\n\n// Second namespace declaration - gets merged\nnamespace MyLib {\n  export function farewell(name: string): void {\n    console.log(`Goodbye, ${name}`);\n  }\n}\n\n// Both functions are available\nMyLib.greet(\"John\");    // \"Hello, John\"\nMyLib.farewell(\"John\"); // \"Goodbye, John\"\n```\n\n**Class and Namespace Merging:**\n```typescript\nclass Calculator {\n  public value: number = 0;\n  \n  add(num: number): this {\n    this.value += num;\n    return this;\n  }\n}\n\n// Namespace merges with the class\nnamespace Calculator {\n  export function multiply(a: number, b: number): number {\n    return a * b;\n  }\n  \n  export const PI = 3.14159;\n}\n\n// Both instance methods and static members are accessible\nconst calc = new Calculator();\ncalc.add(5);\nconsole.log(calc.value); // 5\n\nconsole.log(Calculator.multiply(3, 4)); // 12\nconsole.log(Calculator.PI); // 3.14159\n```\n\n**Enum Merging:**\n```typescript\n// First enum declaration\nenum Color {\n  Red,\n  Green\n}\n\n// Second enum declaration - continues the numbering\nenum Color {\n  Blue = 2,\n  Yellow = 3\n}\n\n// Result: Red = 0, Green = 1, Blue = 2, Yellow = 3\nconsole.log(Color.Red);    // 0\nconsole.log(Color.Green);  // 1\nconsole.log(Color.Blue);   // 2\nconsole.log(Color.Yellow); // 3\n```\n\n**Module Augmentation (Extending External Modules):**\n```typescript\n// In a declaration file (.d.ts)\ndeclare module \"express\" {\n  interface Request {\n    user?: {\n      id: number;\n      name: string;\n    };\n  }\n}\n\n// In your code\nimport express from \"express\";\nconst app = express();\n\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  // Now req.user is available and typed\n  req.user = { id: 1, name: \"John\" };\n  next();\n});\n```\n\n**Global Augmentation:**\n```typescript\n// In a global declaration file\ndeclare global {\n  interface Array<T> {\n    first(): T | undefined;\n    last(): T | undefined;\n  }\n}\n\n// Implementation\nArray.prototype.first = function() {\n  return this.length > 0 ? this[0] : undefined;\n};\n\nArray.prototype.last = function() {\n  return this.length > 0 ? this[this.length - 1] : undefined;\n};\n\n// Usage\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\nconsole.log(numbers.first()); // 1\nconsole.log(numbers.last());  // 5\n```\n\n**Advanced Interface Merging:**\n```typescript\n// Base interface\ninterface Config {\n  apiUrl: string;\n  timeout: number;\n}\n\n// Extended interface\ninterface Config {\n  retries?: number;\n  debug?: boolean;\n}\n\n// Further extension\ninterface Config {\n  headers?: Record<string, string>;\n  auth?: {\n    token: string;\n    type: 'Bearer' | 'Basic';\n  };\n}\n\n// Final merged interface\nclass ApiClient {\n  private config: Config;\n  \n  constructor(config: Config) {\n    this.config = {\n      timeout: 5000,\n      retries: 3,\n      debug: false,\n      ...config\n    };\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Plugin System with Declaration Merging:**\n```typescript\n// Base plugin interface\ninterface Plugin {\n  name: string;\n  version: string;\n  initialize(): void;\n}\n\n// Plugin authors can extend the interface\ninterface Plugin {\n  configure?(config: any): void;\n  destroy?(): void;\n}\n\n// Even more extensions\ninterface Plugin {\n  hooks?: {\n    beforeRequest?: (request: any) => void;\n    afterResponse?: (response: any) => void;\n  };\n}\n\nclass PluginManager {\n  private plugins: Plugin[] = [];\n  \n  register(plugin: Plugin): void {\n    this.plugins.push(plugin);\n    plugin.initialize();\n    \n    // Optional methods are now available\n    if (plugin.configure) {\n      plugin.configure({});\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Rules and Limitations:**\n\n**1. Interface Merging Rules:**\n```typescript\n// Non-function members must be unique\ninterface Box {\n  height: number;\n}\n\ninterface Box {\n  width: number; // OK - different property\n  // height: string; // Error - property 'height' already exists with different type\n}\n\n// Function members are overloaded\ninterface Document {\n  getElementById(elementId: string): HTMLElement | null;\n}\n\ninterface Document {\n  getElementById(elementId: string): HTMLElement | null; // OK - same signature\n  getElementsByName(elementName: string): NodeListOf<HTMLElement>; // OK - different signature\n}\n```\n\n**2. Namespace Merging Rules:**\n- Exported members with the same name will merge\n- Non-exported members are not merged\n- Merged namespaces share the same scope\n\n**3. Class and Namespace Merging:**\n- Namespace must be declared after the class\n- Namespace members become static members of the class\n- Class and namespace must have the same name\n\n**Practical Use Cases:**\n\n**1. Type Definition Files:**\n```typescript\n// types.d.ts - extending library types\ndeclare module \"lodash\" {\n  interface LoDashStatic {\n    customMethod<T>(array: T[], predicate: (item: T) => boolean): T[];\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Configuration Extensions:**\n```typescript\n// Different modules can extend the same config interface\n// user-config.ts\ninterface AppConfig {\n  theme: 'light' | 'dark';\n  language: string;\n}\n\n// admin-config.ts\ninterface AppConfig {\n  adminFeatures: {\n    enableDebugMode: boolean;\n    showAdvancedOptions: boolean;\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**3. API Response Extensions:**\n```typescript\n// Base response\ninterface ApiResponse {\n  status: number;\n  message: string;\n}\n\n// Success response\ninterface ApiResponse {\n  data?: any;\n}\n\n// Error response\ninterface ApiResponse {\n  error?: {\n    code: string;\n    details: any;\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits of Declaration Merging:**\n- **Extensibility**: Extend existing types without modification\n- **Plugin Architecture**: Allow plugins to extend core interfaces\n- **Type Enhancement**: Add properties to external library types\n- **Modular Design**: Split large interfaces across multiple files\n- **Backward Compatibility**: Add new features without breaking existing code\n\n**When to Use Declaration Merging:**\n- Extending third-party library types\n- Creating plugin systems\n- Splitting large interfaces across modules\n- Adding global utility methods\n- Enhancing existing type definitions"
